<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>Corral</displayName>
  <description>Policies for Corral, the process manager</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="Corral">Corral</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_Corral21">Corral 2.1 or later</string>
      <string id="LockSettings">Lock settings</string>
      <string id="LockSettings_Help">If you enable this setting, nobody can change rules, ProBalance, Game Mode or optimizations from the Corral interface, nor change a process priority. The affected pages stay visible as read-only.

Use “Enforced rules” to provide the rules.

Takes effect when the Corral service or the PC restarts.</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_Corral23">Corral 2.3 or later</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_Corral31">Corral 3.1 or later</string>
      <string id="AllowStandardUsers">Allow standard accounts to change settings</string>
      <string id="AllowStandardUsers_Help">By default, only administrators of the PC (including without UAC elevation) can change rules, ProBalance, Game Mode and optimizations, change a process priority or pause Corral. Standard accounts see the interface as read-only: the service acts with system rights on the processes of every session, and a standard account must not be able to use it against other users' programs.

If you enable this setting, standard accounts get these rights back (behavior of versions before 3.1). Only use it on a PC whose users are all trusted. “Lock settings” still takes precedence.

Takes effect when the Corral service or the PC restarts.</string>
      <string id="FleetConfig">Signed fleet configuration</string>
      <string id="FleetConfig_Help">Configuration shared by all computers: a JSON file published on a network share or an https address, signed with your organization's key. The sections it contains (Rules, ProBalance, GameMode) replace the local configuration and can no longer be changed on the computer.

Preparation, with Corral.exe:
  Corral fleet keygen key.pem  (once; keep the private key safe)
  Corral fleet sign config.json key.pem  (after each change; increase "Serial")
Publish config.json and config.json.sig side by side, then enter their location and the public key shown by keygen here.

The service rereads the file every 15 minutes. A badly signed file, or one older than the last received, is refused; off the network, the last valid version stays applied.</string>
      <string id="RulesFile">Enforced rules</string>
      <string id="RulesFile_Help">Path of a rules file exported from Corral (Rules page, ⋯ menu › Export). Its rules replace the local rules on every computer.

The file can be on a network share: it is read by the Corral service (computer account), which must be able to access it. If it cannot be read, the local rules are kept and a warning is written to the Event Viewer.

Environment variables are accepted (%ProgramData%…).</string>
      <string id="ProBalance">ProBalance</string>
      <string id="ProBalance_Help">Enabled: ProBalance is always on. Disabled: ProBalance is always off. Not configured: the user decides.</string>
      <string id="DisableUpdates">Turn off automatic updates</string>
      <string id="DisableUpdates_Help">If you enable this setting, Corral neither checks for nor installs updates: deploy new versions yourself (Corral.msi, silent install with msiexec /i Corral.msi /qn).</string>
      <string id="DisableProcessTermination">Prevent ending processes</string>
      <string id="DisableProcessTermination_Help">If you enable this setting, Corral's “End process” command is refused.</string>
      <string id="DisableStartupManager">Prevent managing startup programs</string>
      <string id="DisableStartupManager_Help">If you enable this setting, Corral's Startup page is read-only.</string>
    </stringTable>
    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="FleetConfig">
        <textBox refId="FleetConfigSource">
          <label>Location (path, share or https):</label>
        </textBox>
        <textBox refId="FleetConfigKey">
          <label>Public key:</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="RulesFile">
        <textBox refId="RulesFilePath">
          <label>Rules file:</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
